---
import ConceptLayout from '../../layouts/ConceptLayout.astro'
import DiagramCascade from '../../components/DiagramCascade.astro'

/** How one flag is decided for one organisation (apps/web/src/permissions/features.ts). */
const evaluation = [
	{ label: 'Release gate', detail: 'dark in this environment → off' },
	{ label: 'Override', detail: 'this organisation was forced on or off' },
	{ label: 'On / Off', detail: 'the platform state, for everyone' },
	{ label: 'Rollout', detail: 'inside the percentage?' },
	{ label: 'Default', detail: 'what the registry entry says' },
]
---
<ConceptLayout
	slug="feature-flags"
	source={[
		'packages/shared/src/features.ts',
		'apps/web/src/permissions/features.ts',
		'apps/web/src/api/middleware/feature.ts',
		'apps/web/src/api/services/features.ts',
		'apps/web/src/ui/lib/feature-guards.ts',
	]}
>
	<h2>Two questions, not one</h2>
	<p>
		"Is this feature on?" is really two questions, and conflating them is how flag systems go
		wrong. <em>Does this surface exist in this deployment at all?</em> is a release decision, made
		when you deploy. <em>Which organisations have it yet?</em> is a rollout decision, made by an
		administrator afterwards, without shipping anything.
	</p>
	<p>
		So there are two layers. The release gate is a single deployment variable listing the keys this
		environment ships — blank means none, which is the direction you want a mistake to fail in.
		The rollout lives in the database: a platform state per flag, plus an override per
		organisation, both edited from the admin area. One function composes them, in this order:
	</p>
	<figure class="figure">
		<DiagramCascade
			tiers={evaluation}
			arrowLabel="first that answers"
			describedBy="flag-evaluation"
			title="How one feature flag is decided for one organisation: the release gate first, then an override, then the platform state"
		/>
		<figcaption>
			The release gate is consulted only for a flag that asked to be gated, so an everyday rollout
			needs no deployment change — only a genuinely dark feature does.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>
	<table id="flag-evaluation">
		<thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What it decides</th></tr></thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td>Release gate</td><td>the flag asked to be environment-gated and this environment does not list it — off, and nothing below is consulted</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Override</td><td>this organisation was forced on or forced off; that beats the percentage in either direction</td></tr>
			<tr><td>On / Off</td><td>the platform state, applying to everyone with no override</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Rollout</td><td>the organisation (or the person) falls inside the percentage, decided by a hash rather than a coin toss</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Default</td><td>no row exists at all, so the flag's own registry entry answers</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<div class="why">
		<p>
			<strong>Why the override beats the rollout.</strong> That is what an override is for: the
			design partner who must have it on day one, and the customer who must not get it at all.
			Being forced is an explicit value on the row and the row's presence is the exception — so the
			admin screen offers On, Off or Default, never a checkbox that cannot express "I have not
			decided".
		</p>
	</div>

	<h2>A flag is not a permission</h2>
	<p>
		This is the load-bearing rule on the page, and it comes from a real incident rather than a
		principle. It is tempting to express a flag as something the permission system can answer —
		the vocabulary is already there, the guards are already written. Do that and a platform
		administrator, whose permissions are a wildcard over everything, satisfies the check for every
		flag that exists.
	</p>
	<p>
		An application built on this kit shipped exactly that. Five routes were open in production to
		platform staff, while the same feature's dashboards and reporting — gated a different way —
		stayed correctly dark. Two sources of truth, disagreeing, in a design whose whole point was to
		have one.
	</p>
	<div class="why">
		<p>
			<strong>So every gate reads the same list.</strong> The resolved flags travel on the
			authenticated context as a plain array of strings, and the server, the browser and the
			command line all ask that array. A wildcard permission cannot widen a string comparison. The
			permission-shaped form still exists for an application that genuinely wants entitlements per
			customer, but nothing that hides an unreleased surface may use it.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h2>Every door, including the three without a nav item</h2>
	<p>
		Hiding the navigation entry is the part everyone remembers. The kit treats a feature as
		something with four doors, and the sharp one is not the nav:
	</p>
	<table>
		<thead><tr><th>Door</th><th>How it is gated</th></tr></thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr>
				<td>API routes</td>
				<td>one optional entry beside the route prefix, so the whole surface is dark at once — declared once, like authentication</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>The analytics layer</td>
				<td>the cube list is filtered per request, so a gated feature's data is not queryable either</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>Dashboard templates</td>
				<td>the one that <em>creates rows</em> — templates seed themselves into every organisation on first load, so an ungated template arrives everywhere the moment you deploy</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>Nav, routes, settings tabs</td>
				<td>a guard that takes a flag and a permission as two separate facts, rather than pretending a flag is a permission</td>
			</tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>
		A gated API route answers 404, not 403. A 403 tells the caller the feature exists and they are
		not allowed it, which is exactly the thing a dark release is trying not to say.
	</p>

	<h2>Keys are code, and the rollout is arithmetic</h2>
	<p>
		A flag key is declared in the shared package beside its description, the same way prompts and
		agents are. Adding one needs no migration; mistyping one in a gate is a type error rather than
		a route that quietly 404s for ever. Evaluation walks the registry, so a database row whose key
		no longer exists is inert by construction — retiring a flag is: remove the gate, deploy, delete
		the line.
	</p>
	<p>
		The cost of that choice, stated plainly: an administrator cannot invent a flag key without a
		deploy. That is deliberate. A flag no code reads does nothing, so the code and its registry
		line ship together.
	</p>
	<p>
		Who falls inside a percentage is a hash of the flag key and the unit — an organisation, or a
		person — rather than a random draw, which buys three properties worth naming:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Raising a percentage only ever adds.</strong> The percentage is not part of what is
			hashed, so nobody who is already inside a rollout falls out of it when you go from 10% to 25%.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Flags do not correlate.</strong> The key is part of what is hashed, so one flag's
			first 10% is not the same 10% as the next flag's — otherwise every early rollout lands on the
			same unlucky customers.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>The same unit always gets the same answer</strong>, on every request and in every
			isolate, with nothing stored.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<div class="note">
		<p>
			That hash is a wire format, not an implementation detail: changing it reshuffles every live
			rollout under way. It is pinned by tests with fixed expected values, so a well-meaning
			improvement fails loudly instead of silently moving customers in and out of a beta.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h2>Resolved with the session, not fetched</h2>
	<p>
		Flags are resolved where the session is resolved — one more part of the query that already
		loads the user, their organisation and their groups — so a request costs nothing extra to know
		what is on. API-key callers read theirs alongside, with no cache, so a rollout reaches a script
		on its very next call.
	</p>
	<p>
		Changing an override for one organisation nudges that organisation, and open browser tabs
		refresh what they can see without a reload. A platform-wide change does not fan out — that
		would be one message per organisation on the platform — so it reaches open sessions the next
		time they ask who they are, which for a rollout is the right trade.
	</p>
	<div class="note">
		<p>
			<strong>Client-side hiding is cosmetic, as everywhere else in the kit.</strong> The gated
			code still ships in the browser bundle and a determined reader can find it. The server is the
			protection: the routes are 404, the data is not queryable, and the dashboards do not exist.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h2>What it is not</h2>
	<p>
		There is no per-person targeting beyond choosing to count people rather than organisations in a
		rollout, no scheduling, and no experiment framework — a flag here answers "who has this yet",
		not "which variant performed better".
	</p>
	<p>
		Cloudflare ships a flag product, and it was considered and turned down for this job: its model
		is your account, your apps, your flags, with no notion of <em>your customers</em>, so
		per-organisation state would have to live as targeting-rule data that an application writes
		through an operator API. A toggle is a whole-object write with no safe concurrent update, the
		audit trail records a token rather than a person, and there is no local store — which would
		break the promise that this kit runs from a clone with nothing configured. It remains the right
		tool for rolling the kit's own code across deployments; that is a different job from deciding
		which of your customers has what.
	</p>
</ConceptLayout>
